items(231,[
I('P000365','theorem','Regular unipotents and the singular locus in type A',r'For \(SL_n(\mathbb C)\), one Jordan block gives a nonsingular point of the variety \(R\) of unipotent elements; several blocks give singular points. The \(SL_2\) example is a quadratic cone with vertex at the identity.',r'For \(G=SL_n(\mathbb C)\), the unipotent variety is isomorphic by \(u\mapsto u-I\) to the nilpotent cone in \(\mathfrak{sl}_n\). Its smooth locus is the regular nilpotent orbit, equivalently the matrices with a single Jordan block. In \(SL_2\), writing \(a=1+x,d=1-x\) gives \(x^2+bc=0\).',r'The regularity framework precedes the seminar: \citet[Theorems 1.1--1.5]{steinberg1965regular}; the notebook applies it in type A. The complex quadratic cone is a complex surface, not literally a real two-dimensional cone.',r'this is a standard established description. The restriction to \(\mathbb C\) avoids inseparability and bad-characteristic issues hidden by the notebook’s broader comment.',refs=['steinberg1965regular']),
I('P000365','construction','Springer’s resolution by invariant complete flags',r'Pairs \((p,g)\), where \(p\in B\backslash G\) and \(pgp^{-1}\in U\), define \(\widetilde R\to R\); the source is smooth and the generic fibre is one point. The margin calls \(B\backslash G\) projective space.',r'For \(G=SL_n(\mathbb C)\), let \(\mathcal B\) be the complete flag variety and \(\widetilde R=\{(u,F_\bullet):uF_i\subset F_i\}\). The projection to the unipotent variety is proper and birational, and \(\widetilde R\simeq G\times^B U\) is smooth. A regular unipotent has a unique invariant complete flag. The base is a projective variety, generally not a projective space.',r'This is the resolution in \citet{springer1969unipotent}, an account from the 1968 Bombay colloquium published in 1969. The original chapter was not obtained; its identity is corroborated by later primary papers, while the construction here is independently checked as an associated bundle.',r'the type-A construction is established. An assertion over every field should be phrased as a geometric morphism after base change, not as a resolution by sets of rational points.',verification='indirect',publication_match='exact',refs=['springer1969unipotent','steinberg1965regular']),
I('P000365-P000366','theorem','Invariant flag counts and Hall polynomials',r'The number of flags over \(\mathbb F_q\) is polynomial in \(q\); for a unipotent \(g\), the fixed-flag count depends only on its Jordan partition. Green’s 1955 character paper is recommended.',r'The number of complete flags in \(\mathbb F_q^n\) is \(\prod_{j=1}^n(1+q+\cdots+q^{j-1})\). For a nilpotent operator of fixed Jordan type \(\lambda\), its invariant complete flags are counted by a polynomial in \(q\). It can be computed by successive Hall-number counts of submodules of the corresponding \(\mathbb F_q[t]\)-module; the total count need not be identified with one unspecified Hall polynomial.',r'The Hall-polynomial and finite-general-linear-group connection was already available in \citet{green1955characters}. A later geometric verification through affine decompositions is read in \citet[Introduction and \S1]{shimomura1980fixed}. Green’s full text was not successfully opened in this run.',r'the counting assertion is established. The field-independent Jordan type and the choice of complete rather than partial flags must be fixed.',refs=['green1955characters','shimomura1980fixed']),
I('P000366','claim','Flag counts and the even Poincaré polynomial',r'The notes compare the finite-field polynomial with the Poincaré polynomial after replacing \(q\) by \(t^2\).',r'The complex full flag variety has an affine Bruhat decomposition with cells \(\mathbb C^{\ell(w)}\), \(w\in S_n\). Thus its cohomology is concentrated in even degrees and \(P(t)=\sum_wt^{2\ell(w)}\), while its split finite-field model has \(\sum_wq^{\ell(w)}\) points. A corresponding comparison for invariant-flag varieties requires a compatible affine paving, not an arbitrary variety whose point count happens to be polynomial.',r'The full-flag comparison is classical. The later explicit invariant-flag decomposition of \citet[\S1]{shimomura1980fixed} substantiates the stronger type-A comparison; the paper credits Spaltenstein’s 1976 complete-flag result.',r'the full-flag statement and the type-A Springer-fibre analogue are established. The notebook does not record a general theorem equating arbitrary point counts and Betti numbers.',refs=['shimomura1980fixed','green1955characters']),
I('P000366','claim','Dimension of a Springer fibre and the centralizer',r'The fibre dimension is bounded above by \(\tfrac12(\dim Z_g-(n-1))\).',r'For unipotent \(u\in SL_n(\mathbb C)\), \(\dim\mathcal B_u=\tfrac12(\dim Z_{SL_n}(u)-(n-1))\). If the Jordan partition is \(\lambda\), this is \(\sum_i(i-1)\lambda_i\). In \(GL_n\), replace \(n-1\) by \(n\), since the centralizer dimension also increases by one.',r'The notebook’s upper bound belongs to Springer’s earlier geometry \citep{springer1969unipotent}; later fixed-flag work supplies sharper structural descriptions \citep[Introduction]{shimomura1980fixed}. The exact original locator for the upper bound was not retrieved, so the historical match is indirect.',r'the equality in type A is established. The rank term is group-dependent; mixing a \(GL_n\) centralizer with the \(SL_n\) rank gives an incorrect half-integer formula.',verification='indirect',refs=['springer1969unipotent','shimomura1980fixed'])
])
items(232,[
I('P000366','remark','The unlocated Rego note on cohomology and integrable distributions',r'The heading says Rosenfeld reports on Rego, \emph{C. R.}, volume 272 (1971), and associates cohomology with an integrable distribution. The author name is legible on the scan.',r'The surviving text does not specify the cochain groups, differential, equivalence relation on distributions, or an exact theorem. It therefore does not determine a unique cohomology theory or a definite open problem.',r'Searches combining Rego, volume 272, 1971, foliation/cohomology, and Frölicher--Nijenhuis terminology did not identify the cited note securely. The surrounding derivation calculus can be identified independently, but it does not justify assigning the missing theorem to a guessed author.',r'the bibliographic identification remains unresolved in this bounded search. No assertion that the idea was undeveloped or remains open is warranted.',**{'class':'F'},literal_correctness='not determinable',publication_match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low',development='none'),
I('P000366','theorem','Decomposition of graded derivations and the Frölicher–Nijenhuis bracket',r'The notes describe derivations of the graded algebra of forms by vector-valued forms and insertion. For a vector-valued \(p\)-form \(L\), they write \(d_L=d\,i_L+(-1)^p i_Ld\).',r'Every degree-\(k\) graded derivation of \(\Omega^*(M)\) has a unique decomposition \(\mathcal L_K+i_L\), with \(K\in\Omega^k(M;TM)\), \(L\in\Omega^{k+1}(M;TM)\), and \(\mathcal L_K=[i_K,d]\). The commutator of Lie derivations defines \([K,H]_{FN}\) by \([\mathcal L_K,\mathcal L_H]=\mathcal L_{[K,H]_{FN}}\).',r'This calculus is due to Frölicher and Nijenhuis in 1956. It is explicitly attributed and reviewed in the author’s account \citet[Introduction]{michor1989derivations}; the original 1956 publisher text was not accessible here.',r'the decomposition is established. The notebook’s \(d_L\) equals \((-1)^p\mathcal L_L\) in this convention; that is a convention difference, not by itself a mathematical error.',reconstruction=r'The form degrees and graded commutator are supplied. For \(L=\operatorname{Id}_{TM}\), the notebook convention gives \(-d\), consistently with this sign conversion.',refs=['michor1989derivations']),
I('P000366','claim','When an induced Lie derivation is a differential',r'The short entry links the construction with integrability and a cohomology, without specifying a degree or a torsion identity.',r'For a vector-valued one-form \(N\), \(\mathcal L_N^2=\tfrac12\mathcal L_{[N,N]_{FN}}\). Hence vanishing Nijenhuis torsion gives a degree-one differential. An arbitrary graded Lie derivation does not automatically square to zero; a foliation gives a leafwise de Rham differential by a different, explicitly specified construction.',r'The precise graded identity belongs to the Frölicher--Nijenhuis calculus reviewed in \citet{michor1987remarks}. The relation of this identity to the unidentified Rego announcement is only a reconstruction, not an exact publication match.',r'the torsion-zero implication is standard. Which of these complexes Rosenfeld intended cannot be decided from this page alone.',reconstruction=r'The vector-valued one-form formulation is a mathematically valid interpretation, not a recovered missing theorem.',publication_match='partial',confidence='medium',refs=['michor1987remarks'])
])
